<<<
school (of fish &c.), shoal. The two words are etymologically one, & equally unconnected with the ordinary word school;
===
**school** (of fish &c.), **shoal.** The two words are etymologically one, & equally unconnected with the ordinary word *school*;
>>>

<<<
sense. The form school has the disadvantage of being liable to be taken for a figurative use of the other school.
===
sense. The form *school* has the disadvantage of being liable to be taken for a figurative use of the other school.
>>>

<<<
sciagraphy &c., ski-, The regular representative in English of Greek sk- (here skia shadow) is se-; but it is legitimate (see GREEK G) to pronounce c as k, ef. sceptic.
===
**sciagraphy &c., ski-,** The regular representative in English of Greek sk- (here *skia* shadow) is sc-; but it is legitimate (see ^^GREEK G^^) to pronounce c as k, cf. *sceptic*.
>>>

<<<
19th as equivalent to radiography (production of Réntgen-ray pictures) &c., usually with the spelling sk-. To maintain both the se- & the sk- forms is very unsatisfactory, &, with radiography in existence, also needless.
===
19th as equivalent to radiography (production of Röntgen-ray pictures) &c., usually with the spelling sk-. To maintain both the sc- & the sk- forms is very unsatisfactory, &, with *radiography* in existence, also needless.
>>>

<<<
It is best to abandon the Réntgen-ray sense, 517
===
It is best to abandon the Röntgen-ray sense,
>>>

<<<
+ restrict the words to their older use in perspective, spell only sc-, & pronounce sk-. sciamachy, sciametry. See GREEK G. science & art. S. knows, a. does; a s. is a body of connected facts, an a.is a set of directions;
===
+ restrict the words to their older use in perspective, spell only sc-, & pronounce sk-.

**sciamachy, sciametry.** See ^^GREEK G^^.

**science & art.** S. knows, a. does; a s. is a body of connected facts, an a. is a set of directions;
>>>

<<<
scilicet, usually shortened to scil. or sc., is Latin (scire licet you may know) for ‘to wit’.
===
**scilicet,** usually shortened to *scil.* or *sc.*, is Latin (*scire licet* you may know) for 'to wit'.
>>>

<<<
It is not so often misused as e.g. & i.e., not having been popularized to the same extent.
===
It is not so often misused as *e.g.* & *i.e.*, not having been popularized to the same extent.
>>>

